  "note": "An advanced voice week at 27 minutes a day, every tool set to the level: the hard leaps (tritone, minor 6th, both 7ths, minor 3rd) are found from a neighbour, then sung down, in any key and with the voice hidden; expert runs in sixteenths state their range; expert tunes from the 7ths, octave and past-the-octave stages are read from the staff with no chords before they are heard. Guide tones are the key skill and return on five days in new forms: two lines over a ii-V-I by a Key selector, round a turnaround, faster by a Stage selector, landing on the 3rd or 7th, and from memory on day 7.",
  "plan": {
    "title": "Week 1: hard leaps, fast runs and lines that follow the chords",
    "summary": "Sing the leaps that are hard to hear, first from a neighbour you know, then in any key without seeing your voice. Each day opens with breath or a slide, has an ear game and a fast run in the middle, and ends with a tune to read or a line of your own over the chords.",
    "progress": "Breath & range: in, hold and out from 6 counts up to 13, a long breath out of 16 counts, octave slides over a drone up to B♭ and a two-octave slide in C. Next: counts up to 15, the octave slide in B and C.\nIntervals & leaps: hears and sings the tritone, the minor 6th and both 7ths up from Do, each found from a neighbour (Sol, the high Do); minor 3rd, tritone and minor 6th down; all of them in any key and without seeing the voice. Hears them falling and sounded together, and 9ths and 10ths. Next: the leaps from notes other than Do, the minor 2nd and major 6th down.\nRuns: pentatonic runs, the chord up to the high Do, the octave run and the scale up to the high Mi, all in sixteenths up to the top of the range. Next: longer runs that join two of these, and runs in a minor key.\nMelodies: read expert tunes with 7ths, octaves, a 9th and a 10th from the staff before hearing them, in D, F, G and A. Next: longer tunes read at a set tempo with the metronome.\nHarmony & improvising: guide tones over ii-V-I in C, F, D and E♭ up to 104 bpm, and round a turnaround in D, G, F and C; own lines that start on a guide tone, land on the 3rd or 7th, and leap an octave. Next: guide tones over a minor ii-V-i and a 12-bar blues.",
